2Fwolf","7.internals\u002F6.wolf",{"id":213,"title":188,"body":214,"description":772,"extension":773,"links":774,"meta":775,"navigation":776,"path":184,"seo":777,"stem":185,"__hash__":778},"docs_en\u002F7.internals\u002F1.index.md",{"type":215,"value":216,"toc":762},"minimark",[217,239,254,259,286,290,358,362,545,603,606,610,729,733],[218,219,220],"note",{},[221,222,223,224,228,229,233,234,238],"p",{},"This section is unlisted: it has an address but is not in the sidebar, the search or ",[225,226,227],"code",{},"llms.txt",", and asks search engines not to index it. It is not private. It is Dokos' working analysis of someone else's software, read from public repositories on ",[230,231,232],"strong",{},"19 November 2026",". Nothing here was checked on a running Frappe Cloud v2 server. Where the repositories' own documents and their code disagree, the code is reported, and the gap is listed in ",[235,236,209],"a",{"href":237},"\u002Finternals\u002Fwolf#where-the-documents-lag-the-code",".",[221,240,241,242,248,249,238],{},"Since ",[235,243,247],{"href":244,"rel":245},"https:\u002F\u002Fgithub.com\u002Fbthuner\u002Fdodock-wolf\u002Fissues\u002F136",[246],"nofollow","#136",", Wolf builds next to Frappe Cloud v2, not over it. This section says what that ground is. The illustrated version is the ",[235,250,253],{"href":251,"rel":252},"https:\u002F\u002Fbthuner.github.io\u002Fdodock-wolf\u002Fmaking-on\u002F",[246],"making-on",[255,256,258],"h2",{"id":257},"in-one-paragraph","In one paragraph",[221,260,261,262,265,266,269,270,273,274,277,278,281,282,285],{},"Frappe Cloud v2 is six applications. ",[230,263,264],{},"Central"," is one global Frappe site: teams, billing, the console. Each region has an ",[230,267,268],{},"Atlas"," (a Frappe site that runs Firecracker virtual machines on physical hosts, with a proxy in front) and a ",[230,271,272],{},"Cargo"," (a Frappe site that builds the images, runs the S3 storage, and runs the region's telemetry store). ",[230,275,276],{},"Datum"," is that store's write API, in front of ClickHouse. ",[230,279,280],{},"Beacon"," is one global Frappe site that reads every region's ClickHouse and pages people. A customer server is one virtual machine that runs ",[230,283,284],{},"Pilot",", which manages the bench and its sites. The applications never share a database: they talk over HTTPS, with short Ed25519 tokens, HMAC-signed webhooks, and the virtual machine's metadata service.",[255,287,289],{"id":288},"three-planes","Three planes",[291,292,293,312],"table",{},[294,295,296],"thead",{},[297,298,299,303,306,309],"tr",{},[300,301,302],"th",{},"Plane",[300,304,305],{},"How many",[300,307,308],{},"Applications",[300,310,311],{},"Owns",[313,314,315,330,344],"tbody",{},[297,316,317,321,324,327],{},[318,319,320],"td",{},"Global",[318,322,323],{},"one site each",[318,325,326],{},"Central, Beacon",[318,328,329],{},"Teams, capabilities, billing, the console; incidents, service levels, paging, the status page",[297,331,332,335,338,341],{},[318,333,334],{},"Region",[318,336,337],{},"one of each per region",[318,339,340],{},"Atlas, Cargo, Datum + ClickHouse, the proxy, the WireGuard gateway, Garage (S3)",[318,342,343],{},"Hosts and virtual machines, networks, routes and the wildcard certificate; images, buckets; telemetry",[297,345,346,349,352,355],{},[318,347,348],{},"Server",[318,350,351],{},"one virtual machine per customer server",[318,353,354],{},"Pilot, the bench, the sites, MariaDB, Redis, nginx",[318,356,357],{},"Apps, sites, backups, updates, custom-domain certificates, every log",[255,359,361],{"id":360},"what-was-read","What was read",[291,363,364,386],{},[294,365,366],{},[297,367,368,371,374,377,380,383],{},[300,369,370],{},"Repository",[300,372,373],{},"Commit",[300,375,376],{},"What it is",[300,378,379],{},"Stack",[300,381,382],{},"Version",[300,384,385],{},"License",[313,387,388,414,446,472,497,522],{},[297,389,390,397,402,405,408,411],{},[318,391,392],{},[235,393,396],{"href":394,"rel":395},"https: